1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,538
You have never seen the real world.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:02,537
Not once.

3
00:00:02,962 --> 00:00:05,275
According to AI researcher Joscha Bach,

4
00:00:05,662 --> 00:00:08,638
everything you experience right now is a simulation,

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,038
built by your brain,

6
00:00:10,425 --> 00:00:11,725
inside a dark skull.

7
00:00:12,138 --> 00:00:13,225
In the next ten minutes:

8
00:00:13,612 --> 00:00:16,250
why our civilization is sliding toward an abyss,

9
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:19,237
why social media is a global brain having

10
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:19,812
a seizure,

11
00:00:20,188 --> 00:00:22,138
what the ancients really meant by spirit,

12
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:23,900
why your self cannot die,

13
00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:26,100
and why happiness is just a cookie your

14
00:00:26,112 --> 00:00:27,438
brain bakes for itself.

15
00:00:27,946 --> 00:00:29,083
We wake up every day,

16
00:00:29,346 --> 00:00:29,933
go to work,

17
00:00:30,321 --> 00:00:33,158
and feel that this routine is absolutely real.

18
00:00:33,583 --> 00:00:35,721
But in his conversation with Lex Fridman,

19
00:00:36,071 --> 00:00:38,858
Joscha Bach describes our existence in a way

20
00:00:38,871 --> 00:00:40,196
that is hard to unsee.

21
00:00:40,621 --> 00:00:42,733
His claim is simple and unsettling:

22
00:00:43,196 --> 00:00:45,096
you have never actually lived in the physical

23
00:00:45,108 --> 00:00:45,433
world.

24
00:00:45,883 --> 00:00:47,146
Think about where your brain is.

25
00:00:47,558 --> 00:00:49,046
It sits in total darkness,

26
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:50,733
sealed inside your skull.

27
00:00:51,158 --> 00:00:52,033
It cannot see,

28
00:00:52,433 --> 00:00:54,521
hear, or touch anything directly.

29
00:00:54,946 --> 00:00:57,296
All it ever receives are electrical signals,

30
00:00:57,558 --> 00:00:59,408
spikes travelling along nerves.

31
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:01,083
So where does the world come from?

32
00:01:01,508 --> 00:01:03,071
The colors, the sounds,

33
00:01:03,308 --> 00:01:05,058
the geometry of the room around you?

34
00:01:05,483 --> 00:01:07,296
Bach's answer is that your brain builds it.

35
00:01:07,708 --> 00:01:09,721
Everything you see is a kind of virtual

36
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:10,333
reality,

37
00:01:10,708 --> 00:01:13,158
a model generated to explain those signals.

38
00:01:13,583 --> 00:01:15,958
Your brain runs something like the physics engine

39
00:01:15,971 --> 00:01:16,908
of a video game,

40
00:01:17,321 --> 00:01:20,183
a fast, simplified simulation of objects,

41
00:01:20,358 --> 00:01:22,733
gravity, faces and space.

42
00:01:23,146 --> 00:01:24,633
It is not a perfect copy of the

43
00:01:24,646 --> 00:01:25,471
outside world.

44
00:01:25,883 --> 00:01:27,396
It is a useful story that lets a

45
00:01:27,408 --> 00:01:28,496
primate survive.

46
00:01:28,908 --> 00:01:30,333
And the physical world itself?

47
00:01:30,783 --> 00:01:33,183
Bach suggests it is something very different from

48
00:01:33,196 --> 00:01:34,108
what we perceive:

49
00:01:34,496 --> 00:01:37,171
a vast pattern of quantum mechanical processes,

50
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:40,046
too fine and too fast to ever experience

51
00:01:40,058 --> 00:01:40,571
directly.

52
00:01:40,983 --> 00:01:43,033
What we call reality is the dream that

53
00:01:43,046 --> 00:01:44,796
our nervous system tells about it.

54
00:01:45,208 --> 00:01:46,183
You are not in the world.

55
00:01:46,596 --> 00:01:48,596
You are in a multimedia novel that your

56
00:01:48,608 --> 00:01:49,846
brain keeps writing,

57
00:01:50,158 --> 00:01:51,071
moment by moment.

58
00:01:51,483 --> 00:01:53,283
So why would our minds build such an

59
00:01:53,296 --> 00:01:54,221
elaborate illusion?

60
00:01:54,633 --> 00:01:56,508
And what does that illusion hide about where

61
00:01:56,521 --> 00:01:57,671
humanity is heading?

62
00:01:58,083 --> 00:01:59,533
We will answer both by the end of

63
00:01:59,546 --> 00:02:00,108
this video.

64
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:01,096
But first,

65
00:02:01,496 --> 00:02:02,883
let's look at what our stories have done

66
00:02:02,896 --> 00:02:03,533
to the planet.

67
00:02:04,046 --> 00:02:06,646
Since the Enlightenment and the Industrial Revolution,

68
00:02:06,958 --> 00:02:08,221
we have felt like gods.

69
00:02:08,646 --> 00:02:09,496
But look at the price.

70
00:02:09,908 --> 00:02:11,008
In roughly a century,

71
00:02:11,246 --> 00:02:13,258
we have burned through fossil fuels that took

72
00:02:13,271 --> 00:02:15,633
the earth a hundred million years to store.

73
00:02:16,046 --> 00:02:17,721
Trees and ancient life,

74
00:02:17,958 --> 00:02:19,008
compressed into carbon,

75
00:02:19,371 --> 00:02:20,908
set on fire in the blink of an

76
00:02:20,921 --> 00:02:21,208
eye.

77
00:02:21,646 --> 00:02:22,796
For thousands of years,

78
00:02:22,996 --> 00:02:25,171
the human population stayed in the hundreds of

79
00:02:25,183 --> 00:02:25,846
millions.

80
00:02:26,258 --> 00:02:28,096
Then it exploded into billions,

81
00:02:28,296 --> 00:02:30,433
because we found a way to borrow energy

82
00:02:30,446 --> 00:02:31,571
from the deep past.

83
00:02:31,996 --> 00:02:34,821
Bach describes our situation with a striking image:

84
00:02:35,121 --> 00:02:36,858
we are skiing down the slope of an

85
00:02:36,871 --> 00:02:37,983
entropic abyss.

86
00:02:38,408 --> 00:02:39,508
We are going fast,

87
00:02:39,721 --> 00:02:40,896
it feels exhilarating,

88
00:02:41,146 --> 00:02:43,233
and almost nobody is asking how the run

89
00:02:43,246 --> 00:02:43,721
ends.

90
00:02:44,133 --> 00:02:45,008
Look at your own life.

91
00:02:45,421 --> 00:02:47,183
You wake up in an air conditioned room,

92
00:02:47,458 --> 00:02:48,833
drive an air conditioned car,

93
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:51,133
and walk into an air conditioned office.

94
00:02:51,546 --> 00:02:52,833
It is a closed cooling chain.

95
00:02:53,246 --> 00:02:55,096
Our food travels the same way,

96
00:02:55,371 --> 00:02:56,671
in refrigerated trucks,

97
00:02:56,896 --> 00:02:58,208
to refrigerated shelves.

98
00:02:58,621 --> 00:03:00,383
If that chain breaks for a few days

99
00:03:00,396 --> 00:03:01,746
in a brutal heat wave,

100
00:03:02,046 --> 00:03:03,546
many people will not survive.

101
00:03:03,958 --> 00:03:05,783
We are far more fragile than we feel.

102
00:03:06,196 --> 00:03:08,596
Our great grandparents knew how to grow food,

103
00:03:08,983 --> 00:03:11,246
mend clothes and survive a hard winter.

104
00:03:11,658 --> 00:03:13,421
Most of us would not last a week

105
00:03:13,483 --> 00:03:15,783
without supply chains we never even see.

106
00:03:16,208 --> 00:03:19,208
In nature, a species that consumes its environment

107
00:03:19,221 --> 00:03:21,296
faster than it can recover behaves like a

108
00:03:21,308 --> 00:03:21,946
parasite,

109
00:03:22,358 --> 00:03:23,633
and it usually collapses.

110
00:03:24,046 --> 00:03:26,258
Our civilization is betting that we can stay

111
00:03:26,271 --> 00:03:27,608
in control of this machine.

112
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:28,871
But in Bach's view,

113
00:03:29,271 --> 00:03:31,083
it is not clear that anyone is steering.

114
00:03:31,508 --> 00:03:33,258
The machine is rolling downhill,

115
00:03:33,546 --> 00:03:35,308
and no one knows which button would stop

116
00:03:35,321 --> 00:03:35,471
it.

117
00:03:35,896 --> 00:03:37,733
So could something smarter than us pull the

118
00:03:37,746 --> 00:03:38,683
emergency brake?

119
00:03:39,096 --> 00:03:42,233
Many people hope that something is artificial intelligence.

120
00:03:42,725 --> 00:03:43,162
For Bach,

121
00:03:43,562 --> 00:03:45,862
AI is much more than automated statistics.

122
00:03:46,288 --> 00:03:49,188
He sees it as humanity's last great philosophical

123
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:49,750
project.

124
00:03:50,162 --> 00:03:51,825
Intelligence, at its core,

125
00:03:52,237 --> 00:03:54,550
is the ability to make models of reality.

126
00:03:54,962 --> 00:03:56,525
And the real goal of AI is a

127
00:03:56,538 --> 00:03:58,237
system that can model itself,

128
00:03:58,612 --> 00:04:00,825
a mind that finally understands what a mind

129
00:04:00,838 --> 00:04:01,150
is.

130
00:04:01,562 --> 00:04:02,250
Seen this way,

131
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:04,750
the Turing test is not really a test

132
00:04:04,762 --> 00:04:06,012
of how clever machines are.

133
00:04:06,425 --> 00:04:07,987
It is a recursive test of us.

134
00:04:08,412 --> 00:04:11,087
Are human beings intelligent enough to understand their

135
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:12,838
own minds well enough to build one?

136
00:04:13,250 --> 00:04:14,688
If we can rebuild ourselves,

137
00:04:14,938 --> 00:04:16,825
we have finally understood ourselves.

138
00:04:17,250 --> 00:04:18,938
But instead of building a wise mind,

139
00:04:19,312 --> 00:04:21,175
we have spent the last decade building a

140
00:04:21,188 --> 00:04:21,738
sick one.

141
00:04:22,175 --> 00:04:24,875
Bach compares social media to a global brain,

142
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:28,300
billions of people wired together into one giant

143
00:04:28,312 --> 00:04:28,825
network.

144
00:04:29,238 --> 00:04:30,275
And what does that brain do?

145
00:04:30,688 --> 00:04:32,288
It has no stable sense of what is

146
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:32,725
real,

147
00:04:33,125 --> 00:04:35,000
no brakes, no one in charge.

148
00:04:35,438 --> 00:04:37,875
He describes Twitter as a global brain in

149
00:04:37,888 --> 00:04:39,300
a kind of permanent seizure,

150
00:04:39,588 --> 00:04:41,762
constantly firing and never settling.

151
00:04:42,175 --> 00:04:44,788
Each of us becomes a little reinforcement learning

152
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,125
agent, chasing the reward of likes,

153
00:04:47,438 --> 00:04:49,350
outrage and empty arguments.

154
00:04:49,762 --> 00:04:51,012
A tiny hit of dopamine,

155
00:04:51,375 --> 00:04:52,412
again and again.

156
00:04:52,838 --> 00:04:54,750
The network learns what keeps us scrolling,

157
00:04:55,112 --> 00:04:56,175
not what keeps us sane.

158
00:04:56,588 --> 00:04:57,138
Meanwhile,

159
00:04:57,525 --> 00:04:58,550
in the physical world,

160
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:01,262
we use clever accounting to drain the planet,

161
00:05:01,575 --> 00:05:02,750
and we call it innovation.

162
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:04,800
A wise global mind would look at the

163
00:05:04,812 --> 00:05:07,362
slope we are skiing down and change course.

164
00:05:07,775 --> 00:05:10,512
Our current global mind is too busy refreshing

165
00:05:10,525 --> 00:05:11,025
its feed.

166
00:05:11,438 --> 00:05:13,562
To understand why we behave this way,

167
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:15,150
Bach goes much deeper,

168
00:05:15,575 --> 00:05:17,500
all the way down to the smallest unit

169
00:05:17,512 --> 00:05:18,050
of life.

170
00:05:18,546 --> 00:05:19,071
What is life,

171
00:05:19,096 --> 00:05:19,396
really?

172
00:05:19,821 --> 00:05:20,833
Bach starts with the cell.

173
00:05:21,246 --> 00:05:23,758
A cell is an extraordinary molecular machine.

174
00:05:24,171 --> 00:05:27,058
It harvests energy from its environment and uses

175
00:05:27,071 --> 00:05:28,983
it to keep its own structure in order,

176
00:05:29,358 --> 00:05:30,908
pushing back against entropy.

177
00:05:31,321 --> 00:05:32,008
In a sense,

178
00:05:32,233 --> 00:05:34,146
a cell is a biological computer,

179
00:05:34,433 --> 00:05:36,296
running a program that keeps it alive.

180
00:05:36,721 --> 00:05:38,921
When billions of cells come together to form

181
00:05:38,933 --> 00:05:39,483
a plant,

182
00:05:39,871 --> 00:05:41,221
an animal or a human,

183
00:05:41,608 --> 00:05:43,221
they need rules to cooperate.

184
00:05:43,646 --> 00:05:45,021
Something has to coordinate them,

185
00:05:45,383 --> 00:05:47,433
so that they act as one organism and

186
00:05:47,446 --> 00:05:48,283
not as a crowd.

187
00:05:48,696 --> 00:05:50,458
And here Bach makes one of his most

188
00:05:50,471 --> 00:05:51,533
surprising claims.

189
00:05:51,958 --> 00:05:54,096
He says that when ancient cultures talked about

190
00:05:54,108 --> 00:05:56,496
spirit, they were pointing at something real.

191
00:05:56,921 --> 00:05:58,658
Spirit is an old word for what we

192
00:05:58,671 --> 00:06:00,308
would now call an operating system.

193
00:06:00,733 --> 00:06:02,783
It is the software that runs an autonomous

194
00:06:02,796 --> 00:06:05,733
robot, the organizing pattern that makes a body

195
00:06:05,746 --> 00:06:06,521
act as one.

196
00:06:06,933 --> 00:06:08,883
A plant has something like a spirit that

197
00:06:08,896 --> 00:06:09,796
links its cells.

198
00:06:10,208 --> 00:06:11,396
An ecosystem has one.

199
00:06:11,808 --> 00:06:12,333
You have one.

200
00:06:12,746 --> 00:06:14,458
And it does not stop at the individual.

201
00:06:14,946 --> 00:06:18,471
Families, companies, religions and nations are higher level

202
00:06:18,483 --> 00:06:19,521
operating systems.

203
00:06:19,933 --> 00:06:21,708
They coordinate millions of people,

204
00:06:22,071 --> 00:06:24,533
the way your mind coordinates billions of cells.

205
00:06:24,946 --> 00:06:26,246
A beehive works like this.

206
00:06:26,658 --> 00:06:27,733
So does a civilization.

207
00:06:28,158 --> 00:06:30,271
We like to think of ourselves as independent

208
00:06:30,283 --> 00:06:32,608
heroes, but in many ways we are cells

209
00:06:32,683 --> 00:06:34,283
inside a superorganism.

210
00:06:34,696 --> 00:06:37,158
And a superorganism does not care about any

211
00:06:37,171 --> 00:06:37,871
single cell.

212
00:06:38,296 --> 00:06:40,121
It cares about what keeps the whole system

213
00:06:40,133 --> 00:06:40,408
running.

214
00:06:40,846 --> 00:06:42,258
Think about how strange that is.

215
00:06:42,683 --> 00:06:43,808
The beliefs you defend,

216
00:06:44,171 --> 00:06:46,071
the rules you follow without thinking,

217
00:06:46,508 --> 00:06:48,221
even the things you find beautiful.

218
00:06:48,633 --> 00:06:50,533
Many of them were installed by the larger

219
00:06:50,546 --> 00:06:51,958
system you were born into.

220
00:06:52,371 --> 00:06:54,571
That raises the most personal question of all.

221
00:06:54,983 --> 00:06:56,883
If you are software running on a primate

222
00:06:56,896 --> 00:06:57,246
body,

223
00:06:57,633 --> 00:06:59,508
what happens to you when the body stops?

224
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,775
The deepest human fear is death.

225
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:04,275
The fear that we will simply disappear from

226
00:07:04,287 --> 00:07:05,037
the universe.

227
00:07:05,462 --> 00:07:07,162
But if reality as we know it is

228
00:07:07,175 --> 00:07:08,625
a model built by the brain,

229
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,600
then the self you cling to is part

230
00:07:10,612 --> 00:07:11,262
of that model.

231
00:07:11,675 --> 00:07:13,950
Your identity is not a physical object.

232
00:07:14,375 --> 00:07:16,375
Bach describes it as a representation,

233
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:18,612
a pattern that your brain creates to keep

234
00:07:18,625 --> 00:07:19,825
track of what it is doing.

235
00:07:20,237 --> 00:07:21,388
Software does not breathe.

236
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:22,850
It has no fixed body.

237
00:07:23,262 --> 00:07:24,088
In that sense,

238
00:07:24,275 --> 00:07:26,512
Bach argues, the self is not the kind

239
00:07:26,525 --> 00:07:28,550
of thing that is born or dies the

240
00:07:28,562 --> 00:07:29,487
way a body is.

241
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:31,750
What ends at death is the process that

242
00:07:31,762 --> 00:07:32,962
keeps telling the story.

243
00:07:33,388 --> 00:07:35,000
He gives a fascinating example:

244
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:36,188
the Dalai Lama.

245
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:37,562
In Tibetan tradition,

246
00:07:37,825 --> 00:07:40,225
the Dalai Lama is not just one man.

247
00:07:40,638 --> 00:07:41,438
It is a role,

248
00:07:41,725 --> 00:07:44,425
an identity, that is deliberately passed on to

249
00:07:44,438 --> 00:07:45,438
the next person,

250
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,188
generation after generation.

251
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:50,338
A kind of software that is consciously reinstalled

252
00:07:50,412 --> 00:07:51,312
into a new body.

253
00:07:51,725 --> 00:07:53,562
And Bach suggests that all of us work

254
00:07:53,575 --> 00:07:54,487
a little like this.

255
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:55,875
You are not your cells.

256
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,425
You are a story your organism tells about

257
00:07:58,438 --> 00:07:58,912
itself.

258
00:07:59,362 --> 00:08:00,237
From this point of view,

259
00:08:00,550 --> 00:08:02,237
the fear of death is the panic of

260
00:08:02,250 --> 00:08:04,388
a program that realizes it will be shut

261
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:06,037
down before its job is done.

262
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:07,475
It is a useful panic.

263
00:08:07,888 --> 00:08:09,237
It keeps the primate alive.

264
00:08:09,662 --> 00:08:11,100
But it is not the final truth about

265
00:08:11,112 --> 00:08:11,725
who you are.

266
00:08:12,138 --> 00:08:14,275
Bach also points out that through meditation,

267
00:08:14,675 --> 00:08:16,575
or through a radical shift of attention,

268
00:08:16,975 --> 00:08:19,787
people can sometimes see this mechanism directly.

269
00:08:20,212 --> 00:08:22,188
They notice that the ego is a tool,

270
00:08:22,425 --> 00:08:24,438
a control model the body uses,

271
00:08:24,675 --> 00:08:26,237
and not the master of the house.

272
00:08:26,650 --> 00:08:27,825
Seeing that can be frightening.

273
00:08:28,237 --> 00:08:29,975
It can also be strangely liberating.

274
00:08:30,479 --> 00:08:32,117
So we arrive at the question we left

275
00:08:32,129 --> 00:08:33,017
open at the start.

276
00:08:33,429 --> 00:08:35,642
If the world we see is a simulation,

277
00:08:36,004 --> 00:08:37,417
if the self is a story,

278
00:08:37,717 --> 00:08:40,267
and if civilization is skiing toward an abyss,

279
00:08:40,667 --> 00:08:42,029
then what is all of this for?

280
00:08:42,442 --> 00:08:43,417
And what is happiness?

281
00:08:43,842 --> 00:08:45,429
Bach's answer begins with suffering.

282
00:08:45,854 --> 00:08:48,279
Suffering happens when your mind cares about things

283
00:08:48,292 --> 00:08:49,279
it cannot change.

284
00:08:49,692 --> 00:08:51,329
It keeps pushing against a wall,

285
00:08:51,629 --> 00:08:52,892
and the wall does not move.

286
00:08:53,304 --> 00:08:53,992
And happiness?

287
00:08:54,417 --> 00:08:56,642
The outside world cannot make you happy,

288
00:08:56,917 --> 00:08:58,929
because the world itself is neutral.

289
00:08:59,342 --> 00:09:00,229
Rain is not sad.

290
00:09:00,642 --> 00:09:01,667
Money is not joyful.

291
00:09:02,079 --> 00:09:03,892
Only your mind decides what things mean.

292
00:09:04,317 --> 00:09:06,117
That is why Bach offers this line,

293
00:09:06,517 --> 00:09:08,117
one of the most memorable of the whole

294
00:09:08,129 --> 00:09:08,892
conversation:

295
00:09:09,354 --> 00:09:11,417
happiness is a cookie that your brain bakes

296
00:09:11,429 --> 00:09:12,129
for itself.

297
00:09:12,579 --> 00:09:14,342
Think of the cookie a parent promises a

298
00:09:14,354 --> 00:09:16,542
child to get them to eat their vegetables.

299
00:09:16,954 --> 00:09:18,892
Your nervous system does the same thing.

300
00:09:19,317 --> 00:09:22,067
Pleasure and satisfaction are signals from your own

301
00:09:22,079 --> 00:09:22,954
operating system,

302
00:09:23,342 --> 00:09:25,729
rewards that keep your primate body fighting,

303
00:09:26,042 --> 00:09:27,192
learning and building.

304
00:09:27,617 --> 00:09:28,817
The cookie was never out there.

305
00:09:29,229 --> 00:09:30,417
It was always baked in here.

306
00:09:30,842 --> 00:09:33,167
This also explains why so many people reach

307
00:09:33,329 --> 00:09:35,667
every goal they set and still feel empty.

308
00:09:36,092 --> 00:09:36,704
The promotion,

309
00:09:37,067 --> 00:09:39,042
the house, the bigger number in the bank

310
00:09:39,054 --> 00:09:39,479
account.

311
00:09:39,892 --> 00:09:41,904
Each one delivers a cookie for a moment,

312
00:09:42,229 --> 00:09:44,354
and then the nervous system raises the bar.

313
00:09:44,779 --> 00:09:46,617
The reward was never in the object.

314
00:09:47,029 --> 00:09:47,817
It was a signal,

315
00:09:48,167 --> 00:09:49,367
designed to keep you moving.

316
00:09:49,792 --> 00:09:51,817
So stop chasing the cookie in the outside

317
00:09:51,829 --> 00:09:52,229
world.

318
00:09:52,642 --> 00:09:55,017
Instead, try to understand your own software.

319
00:09:55,429 --> 00:09:57,442
Notice the stories your mind tells you,

320
00:09:57,829 --> 00:09:59,204
the rewards it dangles,

321
00:09:59,417 --> 00:10:00,617
the fears it runs.

322
00:10:01,042 --> 00:10:03,054
Because once you see the simulation for what

323
00:10:03,067 --> 00:10:05,117
it is, you can stop being a character

324
00:10:05,129 --> 00:10:05,404
in it,

325
00:10:05,792 --> 00:10:07,104
and start becoming its author.

326
00:10:07,517 --> 00:10:09,817
If this conversation bent your mind a little,

327
00:10:10,179 --> 00:10:12,692
watch the full Lex Fridman episode linked in

328
00:10:12,704 --> 00:10:13,392
the description,

329
00:10:13,754 --> 00:10:16,067
and subscribe for more animated breakdowns of the

330
00:10:16,079 --> 00:10:17,904
ideas that shape our future.
